DESCRIPTION

_printf is a simplified version of the standard C printf. It prints formatted output to stdout, supporting a subset of the usual conversion specifiers, plus a few extras like binary.

It keeps an internal buffer of 1024 bytes to reduce the number of system calls, which makes output more efficient.

RETURN VALUE

Returns the number of characters printed. If format is NULL or an error occurs, it returns -1.

FORMAT SPECIFIERS

Here’s what _printf can do:

Specifier	Description
%c	Print a single character.
%s	Print a string. If the string is NULL, prints (nil).
%d, %i	Print a signed decimal integer.
%u	Print an unsigned decimal integer.
%o	Print an unsigned integer in octal.
%x	Print an unsigned integer in lowercase hexadecimal.
%X	Print an unsigned integer in uppercase hexadecimal.
%b	Print an unsigned integer in binary.
%p	Print a pointer (memory address) in hexadecimal with 0x prefix. If pointer is NULL, prints (nil).
%%	Print a literal percent sign.
%r  Print a string in reverse. If the string is NULL, prints (nil)
%R  Print a string in rot13 form. If the string is NULL, prints (nil).
Any other % followed by a character	Prints the literal % and the character (for unknown specifiers).
Note: This printf does not support Flags, Length modifiers, Field width, Percision.

Examples
_printf("Print Char: %c\n", 'A');
_printf("Print String: %s\n", "String");
_printf("Print Int: %i", "100"); 
_printf("Print leteral Percent: %%\n)";

